Why this package is Architecture: all even though it ships a node_modules tree.

The tree is pure JavaScript. Verified, not assumed:

  * `npm ci` from the committed lockfile produces zero compiled addons — checked
    with `find node_modules -name '*.node'` (0 results) and no platform-named
    package directories.
  * build.sh re-checks this on every build and REFUSES to package a payload
    containing an ELF object or a .node addon. If a future dependency pulls in
    native code the build fails, rather than shipping an amd64 payload to arm64
    hosts under an `all` label.

Two lockfile entries look platform-specific and are not:

  * fsevents — macOS only (`os: darwin`), so npm never installs it on Linux.
  * systeminformation — pure JavaScript; it merely declares the platforms it
    supports.

This file is what scripts/check-packaging.sh looks for: a package shipping
node_modules under `Architecture: all` must state, and enforce, why that is true.
